  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nIncreasing Rain Chances Tonight:\n\nAn upper wave currently over the Pacific Northwest is expected \nto eject into the Canadian High Plains through the day today. A\nsurface low pressure center is expected to deepen as is follows\na similar trajectory out of the Northern High Plains. This will\ntighten the pressure gradient across the Upper Midwest, \nincreasing southeasterly winds across the region through the \nday. This will serve to pool moisture across the warm sector,\ninitiating increasing pWats to over 1.5 inches. Showers and \nstorms are expected to start developing along and ahead of the \ncold front late this evening and into the overnight hours as \nlift from both a low level jet and shortwave jet max coincide. \nThese storms could pose a marginal risk for hail and wind into \nthe early morning hours, mainly across Southeast Minnesota and \nWest Central Wisconsin but they should tend to fizzle out with \ntime in the morning as the low level jet weakens. \n\nHighest, Widespread Rain Chances Tuesday:\n\nA short reprieve in shower and storm activity is expected into\nTuesday afternoon as forcing becomes more nebulous and focused\non the mesoscale. Development looks to increase again later \ninto the evening and overnight hours across the southeastern \nhalf of the local area given reinvigoration of the low level \njet the short wave trough axis moving through. There will again\nbe a marginal threat for hail and wind with these storms. Given\nthe elevated instability these storms should be able to tap \ninto modest cloud layer shear. Another concern will be the \nthreat for heavy rainfall later during the day on Tuesday, with\ncontinued southeast winds ahead of the frontal boundary, pWats \nwill continue to climb into the 1.75-2+ inch range. NBM 06.19 \nprobabilities for 1 inch of rain in a 48 hour period ending at 7\nPM Wednesday were between 30-50% across the region, 5-20% for 2\ninches. Overall storm motions seem to be progressive enough to \nkeep flash flooding concerns at bay even though pWats and warm \ncloud depths would suggest potential for highly efficient \nrainfall producers. Rain will gradually push off to the south \nand east through the overnight hours with most places looking to\nbe dry by 12Z Wednesday. \n\nDrying Out, Near Normal Temperatures Into Weekend:\n\nAs the upper wave/trough axis move east, the trailing frontal \nboundary from the occluding surface low well to our north in Canada \nwill sweep through the region early Wednesday. This will finally \nusher in a drier airmass to the region as a whole with dew points \nfalling back into the upper 50s. A weak surface high is expected to \nbuild into the Midwest by Thursday. This, in combination with zonal \nflow to weak ridging aloft, will keep conditions dry for the late \nweek period. Temperatures behind the front won't be much \"cooler\" \nbut they will be a bit closer to normal in the 70s to lower 80s \nThursday and Friday. The next chances for rain look to come by the \nweekend as another upper wave looks to potentially swing through the \nupper Midwest.",
